/-!
# Approximate agreement under common `p`-belief

An agent with information partition `P` and prior `μ` *`p`-believes* an event `E` at `ω` when
the posterior of `E` at `ω` is at least `p`. An event is *evident `p`-belief* for the agent if
the agent `p`-believes it whenever it occurs. An event `C` is *common `p`-belief* at `ω` for a
family of agents if some event containing `ω` is evident `p`-belief for every agent and implies
that every agent `p`-believes `C`.

Monderer and Samet introduce common `p`-belief as an approximation to common knowledge.
With a common prior, posteriors of a measurable event that are common `p`-belief at some state
differ by at most `2 * (1 - p)`. All partitions are measurable with parts of positive prior
probability, the prior need only be finite, and `0 < p`. Thresholds and posteriors are values in
`ℝ≥0∞`; the bound is stated for their real parts.

Theorem 8.1 of Chen et al. assumes a finite nonempty family of agents and a measurable witness
to common `p`-belief. As in Appendix B, `IsCommonBeliefAt` allows any agent index type and
an arbitrary witness set. The proof constructs a measurable witness for each pair of agents.

## Main definitions

* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.belief`: the event that an agent `p`-believes a given event.
* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.IsEvidentBelief`: an event that an agent `p`-believes whenever it occurs.
* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.IsCommonBeliefAt`: common `p`-belief of an event at a state.

## Main statements

* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.belief_belief_subset`: for `0 < p`, `p`-believing that one
  `p`-believes an event implies `p`-believing the event.
* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.IsEvidentBelief.abs_toReal_sub_le`: on an evident `p`-belief on which
  an agent's posterior of `E` is constantly `r`, the probability of `E` conditional on that event
  is within `1 - p` of `r`.
* `MathEconLib.Epistemic.abs_toReal_sub_le_of_isCommonBeliefAt`: posteriors that are common
  `p`-belief differ by at most `2 * (1 - p)`.

## References

Dov Monderer and Dov Samet (1989), *Approximating Common Knowledge with Common Beliefs*,
*Games and Economic Behavior* **1**(2), 170–190.
[DOI: 10.1016/0899-8256(89)90017-1](https://doi.org/10.1016/0899-8256(89)90017-1).

Ruize Chen, Ben Eltschig, Scott Duke Kominers, Ken Ono, and Jujian Zhang (2026),
*We Can't Agree to Disagree, Formally: Aumann's Theorem and Assumption Accounting in Lean*,
Theorem 8.1 and Appendix B.
[DOI: 10.2139/ssrn.6837298](https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6837298).

